              "end": 26,
              "start": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Supreme Court granted certiorari on a limited question whether a securities\u2011fraud complaint that relies only on a few adverse drug\u2011event reports can establish materiality and scienter without statistical significance."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Jonathan Hacker (Petitioners)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Jonathan Hacker (Petitioners), according to Jonathan Hacker (Petitioners), petitioners argue that anecdotal adverse\u2011event reports are insufficient to establish reliable facts about a drug\u2019s safety, especially when only a handful of reports exist among millions of units sold."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2bc59de7d107-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 48,
              "start": 48
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Respondents, supported by the United States amicus, contend that materiality should be judged by whether a reasonable investor would view the information as meaningfully altering the total mix of information, not by a categorical rule requiring statistical significance."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
